    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="takeaways-title">
      <h2 id="takeaways-title">Key takeaways</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Farming changed everything. The Neolithic revolution in the Fertile Crescent and Anatolia created the settled household, stored wealth, and the population growth that made civilization possible.</li>
        <li>Civilization grew ring by ring. On Crete we can watch it widen: household, then clan (the shared tholos tombs), then town, then palace-state.</li>
        <li>The palaces were economic centers. Minoan palaces gathered and redistributed produce, supported crafts, and ran trade, and needed writing (Linear A) to keep accounts.</li>
        <li>Minoan Crete reached a high level of art and engineering, but much remains unknown, including its language and who ruled.</li>
        <li>Civilizations can be damaged, taken over, and lost. Earthquake, possibly the Thera eruption, destruction around 1450 BC, Mycenaean takeover, and the wider collapse around 1200 BC ended Bronze Age Crete.</li>
        <li>Memory outlived the civilization. Greek myths of Minos and the Minotaur kept a distorted memory alive, and Homer’s world grew from the Mycenaean heirs of Crete.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="summary">
        This is not yet a layer of the man. It is the ground under the first
        layer. The next lesson sets that ground in the world Homer remembers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="takeaways" aria-labelledby="failed-title">
      <h2 id="failed-title">What failed, and what the next age learned</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Centralization was brittle.</strong> When so much depended on a
          few palaces that collected and redistributed goods, the destruction of
          those centers brought down the whole system. Carried forward: a society
          needs strength spread across households and communities, not concentrated
          in one place. The Greek polis, with its citizen-farmers, was in part a
          different answer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Wealth without defense invited takeover.</strong> However we
          interpret the scarcity of war in Minoan art, Crete’s wealth ended up in
          the hands of the more warlike Mycenaeans. Carried forward: prosperity
          must be protected. Homer’s world begins with warriors, and the man’s
          first layer is the courage to defend what he values.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Civilization can be lost.</strong> Writing, palaces, and trade
          networks disappeared for centuries after about 1200 BC. Carried forward:
          nothing achieved is permanent. Each generation must learn, keep, and
          pass on what it has received, which is the purpose of an education like
          this one.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="word-day" aria-labelledby="glossary-title">
      <h2 id="glossary-title">Glossary</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Neolithic:</strong> the “new stone age,” the period of the first farming societies.</li>
        <li><strong>Neolithic revolution:</strong> the shift from hunting and gathering to farming and herding.</li>
        <li><strong>Fertile Crescent:</strong> the arc of land from the Levant through southeastern Turkey to Mesopotamia where farming first developed.</li>
        <li><strong>Anatolia:</strong> the Asian part of modern Turkey.</li>
        <li><strong>Bronze Age:</strong> the period when bronze (copper plus tin or arsenic) was the main metal for tools and weapons; in the Aegean, roughly 3100–1100 BC. That span is debated.</li>
        <li><strong>Minoan:</strong> modern name, from King Minos, for the Bronze Age civilization of Crete.</li>
        <li><strong>Mycenaean:</strong> the Bronze Age civilization of mainland Greece, named after Mycenae.</li>
        <li><strong>Tholos tomb:</strong> a round, stone-built tomb; on Crete, used by a group over many generations.</li>
        <li><strong>Palace:</strong> in Minoan archaeology, a large complex around a central court that served as an economic, religious, and administrative center.</li>
        <li><strong>Redistribution:</strong> a system in which produce is collected by a center and then handed out to support workers, rituals, and trade.</li>
        <li><strong>Pithos</strong> (plural pithoi): a large clay storage jar.</li>
        <li><strong>Linear A:</strong> an undeciphered Minoan script.</li>
        <li><strong>Linear B:</strong> a script adapted from Linear A to write early Greek; deciphered by Michael Ventris in 1952.</li>
        <li><strong>Fresco:</strong> painting on wet plaster.</li>
        <li><strong>Labrys:</strong> a double axe, a Minoan sacred symbol.</li>
        <li><strong>Peak sanctuary:</strong> a mountaintop shrine.</li>
        <li><strong>Keftiu:</strong> an Egyptian name for a foreign land, generally identified with Crete. The identification is debated.</li>
        <li><strong>Neopalatial:</strong> the “new palace” period of Crete, about 1700–1450 BC. Those dates are debated.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

